58 (support p890) — sourced to [p."},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u32","old":"End of life: delirium in >80% of patients; for refractory agitated delirium, lorazepam 0.5–1.0 mg PO/IV/SQ is the sedation agent of choice (p890).","new":"End of life: delirium in >80% of patients; if sedation is indicated for severe agitated delirium, lorazepam, starting dose 0.5–1.0 mg PO/IV/SQ is the sedation agent of choice (p890)."},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u98","old":"What agent and dose are recommended for sedation in refractory agitated delirium at the end of life?","new":"What agent and starting dose are recommended for sedation in severe agitated delirium at the end of life?"},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u13","old":"Screening tools (Table 58-3, p884): 4AT scores 0–12, ≥4 suggests delirium; Nu-DESC scores 0–10.","new":"Screening tools (Table 58-3, p884): 4AT scores 0–12, ≥4 suggests delirium; Nu-DESC scores 0–10; Current practice (not the book): when delirium indicators are present, a competent practitioner should assess with 4AT; use CAM-ICU or ICDSC in critical care or the recovery room after surgery; a healthcare professional with relevant expertise makes the final diagnosis if assessment indicates delirium - NICE CG103, recommendations updated 2023, 1.6.1-1.6.2, p11"},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u18","old":"Beers-listed culprits (Table 58-2, p883): anticholinergics, antipsychotics, benzodiazepines, corticosteroids, H2-blockers, meperidine, Z-drugs (zolpidem/eszopiclone/zaleplon), TCAs. Psychoactive drugs should be discontinued or minimized whenever possible (p885).","new":"Beers-listed culprits (Table 58-2, p883): anticholinergics, antipsychotics, benzodiazepines, corticosteroids, H2-blockers, meperidine, Z-drugs (zolpidem/eszopiclone/zaleplon), TCAs. Psychoactive drugs should be discontinued or minimized whenever possible (p885); Required reading: see AGS Beers 2023 required-article card for opioids and delirium, including the balanced pain-management caveat (Table 3, p2066)"},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u15","old":"Precipitating (Table 58-1, p882): polypharmacy, restraints, bladder catheter, infection, iatrogenic complications, surgery, urgent/trauma admission, coma, ICU stay >10 days, and physiologic derangements (elevated BUN/creatinine ratio, abnormal Na/glucose/K, metabolic acidosis).","new":"Precipitating (Table 58-1, p882): polypharmacy, restraints, bladder catheter, infection, iatrogenic complications, surgery, urgent/trauma admission, coma, ICU stay >10 days, and physiologic derangements (elevated BUN/creatinine ratio, elevated serum urea, abnormal serum albumin, abnormal Na/glucose/K, metabolic acidosis)."},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u62","old":"CHAPTER 58s / HAZZARD","new":"CHAPTER 58 / HAZZARD"},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u72","old":"Exam traps / book contradictions","new":"Exam traps","edits":[[10,32,10,10]]},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u100","old":"The actual 2026 specialist-exam questions and answer keys sourced to this chapter, reproduced verbatim as printed in the full chapter file.","new":"These English translations cover the 2026 exam questions on delirium.","edits":[[3,3,3,5],[4,4,6,16],[5,5,17,27],[6,6,28,33],[7,10,34,36],[16,27,42,42],[42,43,57,58],[44,44,59,60],[45,50,61,61],[51,71,62,63],[72,107,64,64],[109,122,66,66],[123,138,67,68]]},{"chapter":"58","id":"u404","old":"The pages they were sourced to are flagged inline with ⟨2026⟩.","new":"The questions cover the clinical points on those pages.","edits":[[2,2,2,47],[9,61,54,54]]},{"chapter":"58","id":"u602","old":"Note — the book against itself.","new":"Clinical distinction.","edits":[[0,19,0,2],[21,21,4,11],[23,24,13,13],[25,25,14,16],[26,30,17,20]]},{"chapter":"58","id":"u605","old":"All as printed; not reconciled here.","new":"The two descriptions use different time windows.","edits":[[0,3,0,3],[4,5,4,19],[7,9,21,26],[10,32,27,29],[35,35,32,47]]},{"chapter":"58","id":"u620","old":"Note — the book against itself.","new":"Clinical distinction.","edits":[[0,19,0,2],[21,21,4,11],[23,24,13,13],[25,25,14,16],[26,30,17,20]]},{"chapter":"58","id":"u674","old":"Note — the book against itself.","new":"Clinical distinction.","edits":[[0,19,0,2],[21,21,4,11],[23,24,13,13],[25,25,14,16],[26,30,17,20]]},{"chapter":"58","id":"u676","old":"Both are as printed and are not reconciled here.","new":"The figure gives a single starting dose and the text gives a range.","edits":[[0,3,0,1],[4,4,2,3],[5,6,4,8],[9,10,11,15],[12,14,17,20],[16,16,22,27],[17,17,28,38],[18,19,39,39],[24,26,44,46],[28,30,48,51],[32,32,53,61],[33,36,62,63],[37,46,64,65]]},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u33","old":"Acute-onset timeframe: Cardinal Features/Table 58-4 say \"hours to days,\" but the Algorithm-for-Evaluation text says \"hours to weeks\" for acute change — both as printed, not reconciled; a 2–3-week history falls inside one window and outside the other (p886).","new":"Acute-onset timeframe: Cardinal Features/Table 58-4 say \"hours to days,\" but the Algorithm-for-Evaluation text says \"hours to weeks\" for acute change — the two time windows differ; a 2–3-week history falls inside one window and outside the other (p886).","edits":[[152,154,152,152],[156,156,154,155],[157,158,156,171],[160,161,173,178],[162,183,179,179]]},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u35","old":"Risperidone dose: Figure 58-2 gives 0.25 mg; the Antipsychotics text gives 0.25–0.5 mg — both as printed (p889).","new":"Risperidone dose: Figure 58-2 gives 0.25 mg; the Antipsychotics text gives 0.25–0.5 mg (p889).","edits":[[87,105,87,87]]},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u191","old":"Its bibliography prints Table 858-4.","new":"Table 858-4 is a misnumbering in the exam reference.","edits":[[0,24,0,0],[35,35,11,51]]},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u192","old":"The comparison on the cited page is Table 58-4 (p883, 885–886).","new":"The comparison on the page is Table 58-4 (p883, 885–886).","edits":[[21,27,21,21]]},{"chapter":"58s","id":"u214","old":"Its bibliography names p880, where the network hypothesis begins.","new":"The network hypothesis begins on p880.","edi